const studyData = [
  /* ---------------- 1. HCI FOUNDATIONS ---------------- */
  {
    section: "HCI Foundations", id: "sec-foundations", icon: "home", scope: "in",
    blurb: "What HCI is, what good and bad UX looks like, and how interaction has changed over time.",
    items: [
      { id:"f-what-hci", title:"What is HCI?", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>HCI (Human-Computer Interaction)</b> is about designing systems for people by working in quick loops, so problems are found early instead of late.</p>
        <h5>The core cycle</h5><ul><li><b>Design</b> - plan and picture the interaction.</li><li><b>Implementation</b> - build the design.</li><li><b>Evaluation</b> - test it, then feed what you learn back into design.</li></ul>
        <p>This cycle repeats again and again to catch problems as soon as possible.</p>
        <div class="ex">Key question HCI answers: what makes a design good, what makes it bad, and the one thing central to all of it - the <b>user</b>.</div>` },
      { id:"f-ucd-intro", title:"User-Centered Design (UCD)", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>The central factor in all interaction design is the <b>user</b>. UCD means designing with real people in mind.</p>
        <h5>What UCD asks you to do</h5><ul><li>Let people's tasks, goals and values drive what you build.</li><li>Work <i>with</i> users through the whole process, not just at the end.</li><li>Judge every decision from the user's point of view and situation.</li><li>Pay attention to people's abilities and the context they work in.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"f-good-bad-ux", title:"Good UX vs Bad UX", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Good UX feels</h5><ul><li>Useful, easy to learn, helpful, connected, satisfying, accessible, attractive, fun, delightful.</li></ul>
        <h5>Bad UX feels</h5><ul><li>Stressful, inefficient, confusing, distracting, ugly, tedious, inconsiderate, frustrating, condescending.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">These adjective lists help a team name the feeling they want, then design toward it.</div>` },
      { id:"f-why-ux", title:"Why UX Matters", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Success happens where two things overlap: <b>what users want to do</b> and <b>what you want users to do</b>. That overlap is <b>Experience Success</b>.</p>
        <h5>It leads to</h5><ul><li>Meeting product goals.</li><li>Repeat usage.</li><li>Users recommending it to others.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"f-ux-lifecycle", title:"UX Covers the Whole Lifecycle", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>UX is every touchpoint, not just active use: choosing the product, getting it, learning it, using it, fixing it, and upgrading it.</p>
        <div class="ex">Garrett: every product used by someone has a user experience - even a ketchup bottle. Important rule: you cannot design a user experience, you can only <b>design for</b> one.</div>` },
      { id:"f-interaction-design", title:"Interaction Design (the umbrella)", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Interaction Design</b> is building interactive products that are usable, easy to learn, effective and enjoyable.</p>
        <p>It is the <b>umbrella term</b> over UI design, UX design, web design, product design and user-centered design.</p>
        <h5>Roles in the field</h5><ul><li><b>Interaction Designers</b> - design all the interactive parts.</li><li><b>Usability Engineers</b> - evaluate products with usability methods.</li><li><b>Web Designers</b> - build the visual design of sites.</li><li><b>Information Architects</b> - plan and structure products.</li><li><b>UX Designers</b> - do all of the above plus field studies.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"f-usability-vs-ux", title:"Usability vs User Experience", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Usability (ISO 9241-11):</b> how well specified users reach specified goals with <b>effectiveness, efficiency and satisfaction</b> in a context.</p>
        <p>Usability is <b>necessary but not enough</b>. A clean sink and a luxury sink are both "usable", but the experience differs hugely.</p>
        <h5>Relationship</h5><ul><li>Usability covers the period <b>during use</b>.</li><li>UX extends <b>before and after</b> use too.</li><li>Usability is a <b>subset of UX</b>; both sit inside <b>User-Centered Design</b>.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"f-ux-goals", title:"Usability & UX Goals", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Usability goals (easier to measure)</h5><ul><li>Effective, efficient, safe, good utility, easy to learn, easy to remember.</li></ul>
        <h5>UX goals (harder to measure, more emotional)</h5><ul><li>Desirable: satisfying, fun, engaging, pleasurable, rewarding, motivating.</li><li>Avoid: boring, frustrating, patronizing, childish, gimmicky.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">There can be trade-offs - for example, can a product be both fun and safe at the same time?</div>` },
      { id:"f-paradigms", title:"HCI Paradigm Timeline", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>How people interact with computers keeps changing, and each shift redefines "good design".</p>
        <h5>The 7 paradigms</h5><ul>
        <li><b>1. Batch processing</b> - submit, wait, get output (punch cards). No feedback, no interaction.</li>
        <li><b>2. Command line (CLI)</b> - text commands (DOS, Linux). Expert only, heavy memorisation, low discoverability.</li>
        <li><b>3. GUI / WIMP</b> - Windows, Icons, Menus, Pointer. Visuals replace memory; affordances and direct manipulation begin.</li>
        <li><b>4. Web & Hyperlink</b> - navigation-based. Information architecture and consistency become critical.</li>
        <li><b>5. Touch & Mobile</b> - finger replaces pointer. Target size matters; context-aware UX (location, time, behaviour, orientation).</li>
        <li><b>6. Voice & Conversational</b> - natural language (Alexa, Siri). The interface disappears; no visual affordance; error recovery is hard.</li>
        <li><b>7. AI & Adaptive</b> - the system adapts to you (ChatGPT, recommenders). Trust, transparency, explainability and ethics become key.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Big idea: UX rules are not static - they evolve with each interaction paradigm.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 2. COGNITIVE MODELS ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Human Cognition & Cognitive Models", id: "sec-cognition", icon: "brain", scope: "in",
    blurb: "How people perceive, remember, attend, and make errors - and Norman's model for diagnosing problems.",
    items: [
      { id:"c-perception", title:"Perception & Gestalt Principles", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p><b>Perception</b> is how we interpret what we sense. We do not see reality - we see <b>interpreted reality</b>, shaped by context.</p>
        <h5>Key ideas</h5><ul><li><b>Pattern recognition</b> - we read patterns, icons and logos faster than text.</li><li><b>Contrast & visibility</b> - low contrast and tiny links hurt; build a clear visual hierarchy and clear affordances.</li></ul>
        <h5>Gestalt grouping principles</h5><ul>
        <li><b>Proximity</b> - things placed close together feel like a group.</li>
        <li><b>Similarity</b> - things that look alike feel related.</li>
        <li><b>Closure</b> - the mind fills gaps to see a complete shape (a dashed circle looks whole).</li>
        <li><b>Continuation</b> - the eye follows lines and paths.</li>
        <li><b>Figure/Ground</b> - we separate a foreground item from the background (a modal over a dimmed page).</li>
        <li><b>Pragnanz</b> - complex images are reduced to the simplest form.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"c-memory", title:"Memory: Recognition vs Recall", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Two memory stores</h5><ul><li><b>Short-term / working memory</b> - holds about 4 to 7 items, lasts seconds, easily overloaded.</li><li><b>Long-term memory</b> - stores knowledge, skills and habits; persistent.</li></ul>
        <h5>Recognition vs Recall</h5><ul><li><b>Recognition</b> - identifying something shown to you (pick from a menu or list). Easier.</li><li><b>Recall</b> - retrieving from memory with no cue (type a command). Harder.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Design rule: prefer recognition over recall. Command-line interfaces are hard for beginners because they force recall and syntax memory with no recognition support. Selecting an error from a list beats typing an error code.</div>` },
      { id:"c-attention", title:"Attention", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>People have limited focus - they filter, they cannot process everything.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Selective attention</b> - focusing on one thing (reading while ignoring ads).</li><li><b>Divided attention</b> - multitasking, which drops performance (driving while texting).</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Designs that break attention: too many popups, competing alerts, several animations at once.</div>` },
      { id:"c-cog-load", title:"Cognitive Load (Intrinsic, Extraneous, Germane)", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Cognitive load</b> is the total mental effort a task needs. UX aims to cut the effort that does not help.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Intrinsic load</b> - difficulty built into the task itself (learning recursion). Cannot be removed, only simplified and guided.</li>
        <li><b>Extraneous load</b> - effort added by <b>bad design</b> (clutter, confusing labels). This is what designers must remove.</li>
        <li><b>Germane load</b> - <i>helpful</i> mental effort used for learning and forming understanding. Good design supports it.</li></ul>
        <h5>Ways to reduce load</h5><ul><li><b>Chunking</b> - break info into small groups (phone-number formatting).</li><li><b>Progressive disclosure</b> - show advanced options only when needed.</li><li><b>Familiar patterns</b> - reuse common conventions.</li><li><b>Minimise choices</b> - too many options overwhelm (Miller's Law, 7 plus or minus 2).</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Multi-page checkout beats one giant form because it lowers how much you hold in working memory at once.</div>` },
      { id:"c-mental-models", title:"Mental Models vs System Models", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Mental model</b> = the user's idea of how the system works. <b>System model</b> = how it actually works. When they do not match, errors happen.</p>
        <h5>Mismatch examples</h5><ul><li>User thinks Trash deletes forever; system moves it to a recycle bin.</li><li>User drags a file out of a folder expecting a copy; system moves it.</li></ul>
        <p><b>Jakob's Law:</b> users spend most time on <i>other</i> sites, so they expect yours to work like those. <b>Strategy:</b> match the existing mental model, or introduce new ideas gradually with onboarding.</p>
        <div class="ex">The desktop metaphor (files, folders, trash can) succeeded because it matched people's existing model of a physical office - no new model needed.</div>` },
      { id:"c-errors", title:"Error Patterns: Slips, Mistakes, Lapses", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul>
        <li><b>Slips (execution errors)</b> - right intention, wrong action, due to attention failure. Clicking Delete instead of Save. Sub-types: capture, description, mode errors.</li>
        <li><b>Mistakes (decision errors)</b> - wrong plan from a wrong mental model. Choosing the wrong menu, misreading an icon. Rule-based or knowledge-based; harder to detect.</li>
        <li><b>Lapses (memory errors)</b> - forgetting a step. Forgetting to attach a file; skipping a required field.</li></ul>
        <h5>Prevention</h5><ul><li>Slips - separate dangerous actions, add confirmation.</li><li>Mistakes - match mental models, clear labels and icons.</li><li>Lapses - reminders, progress indicators, validation.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">If many users repeat the same error, the <b>design is wrong</b>, not the users. <b>Undo</b> is powerful because it recovers from all three error types and lowers the cost of exploring.</div>` },
      { id:"c-norman-cycle", title:"Norman's 7-Stage Action Cycle", priority:"high", difficulty:"Hard", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p>Every interaction follows a psychological process. Every usability problem maps to one specific stage.</p>
        <h5>The 7 stages</h5><ul><li>1. Form goal - decide what you want.</li><li>2. Form intention - decide to act.</li><li>3. Specify action - decide what to do.</li><li>4. Execute action - do it.</li><li>5. Perceive system state - see what happened.</li><li>6. Interpret system state - understand it.</li><li>7. Evaluate outcome - compare result to the goal.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">With this model designers can diagnose problems systematically, find where cognition breaks, and justify fixes - instead of guessing from opinion or aesthetics.</div>` },
      { id:"c-gulfs", title:"Gulf of Execution & Gulf of Evaluation", priority:"high", difficulty:"Hard", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<ul>
        <li><b>Gulf of Execution</b> - gap between what the user wants and what the system lets them do. The "Where is the button?" problem. Hits stages 2, 3, 4 (intention, specify, execute).</li>
        <li><b>Gulf of Evaluation</b> - gap between the system's output and the user's understanding. The "I clicked it but nothing happened" problem. Hits stages 5, 6, 7 (perceive, interpret, evaluate).</li></ul>
        <p><b>Solution:</b> reduce both with clear controls (execution) and clear feedback (evaluation).</p>
        <div class="ex">Which is worse? A hard-to-find button stops the task starting; unclear feedback can cause repeated actions, duplicates and anxiety - often argued to be worse because users may redo irreversible actions.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 3. USER-CENTERED DESIGN & UX PROCESS ---------------- */
  {
    section: "User-Centered Design & UX Process", id: "sec-ucd", icon: "refresh", scope: "in",
    blurb: "The iterative cycle that drives all UX work, plus Lean UX and how research and design methods map onto it.",
    items: [
      { id:"u-essence", title:"The Essence of UCD", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>User-Centered Design</b> is an iterative process where designers focus on users and their needs at every phase, involving users throughout with research and design techniques to build usable, accessible products.</p>` },
      { id:"u-process", title:"The UX Process Overview", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A cyclical workflow: <b>Strategy</b> (direction) - <b>Research</b> (interviews, observation, surveys) - <b>Analysis</b> (personas, scenarios) - <b>Design</b> (wireframes, prototypes) - <b>Production</b> (visual design, dev, testing) - <b>Evaluate</b> - <b>Beta Launch</b> - <b>Launch</b>.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Strategy</b> sets goals and how success is measured.</li><li><b>Research</b> (Discovery) gathers user and competitor data; often skipped in Lean UX even though it matters.</li><li><b>Analysis</b> turns data into the "why".</li><li><b>Production</b> fleshes out hi-fidelity designs and validates with users.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"u-iterative", title:"Iterative Design: Assess - Design - Build", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p>The core loop repeats until the product is ready.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Assess / Research</b> - understand what users do now, their needs, the design space, and their problems.</li><li><b>Design</b> - take what you learned and generate lots of ideas.</li><li><b>Build</b> - turn ideas into prototypes you can test and get feedback on.</li><li><b>(Re)-Assess</b> - test again, take what you learn, and repeat the whole cycle.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">The Assess phase uses UX <b>research</b> methods; the Design phase uses UX <b>design</b> methods.</div>` },
      { id:"u-lean", title:"Lean UX: Think - Make - Check", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Lean UX runs on a simple three-step loop:</p><ul><li><b>Think</b> - form hypotheses and ideas.</li><li><b>Make</b> - build something to test the idea.</li><li><b>Check</b> - validate against user needs.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Note: Lean UX often undervalues or skips the research phase even though it is important.</div>` },
      { id:"u-methods", title:"Research vs Design Methods", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>UX Research methods (Assess)</h5><ul><li>Interviews, observations, surveys, user testing, inspection methods.</li></ul>
        <h5>UX Design methods (Design)</h5><ul><li>Personas, scenarios, user stories; sketching and ideation; storyboarding; mapping and navigation; comparative research; lo/mid/hi-fidelity prototyping.</li></ul>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 4. UX RESEARCH METHODS & COMPONENTS ---------------- */
  {
    section: "UX Research Methods & Components", id: "sec-research", icon: "search", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Ask, Observe, Inspect - and the four components plus the Honeycomb that define a complete experience.",
    items: [
      { id:"r-aoi", title:"Three Methods: Ask, Observe, Inspect", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Ask</b> - learn users' views by asking directly (interviews, surveys, focus groups, diary studies, experience sampling).</li>
        <li><b>Observe</b> - watch users interact (ethnographic observation, user testing, usage analytics, video analysis, social media mining).</li>
        <li><b>Inspect</b> - judge a design against guidelines (guideline-based review, walkthroughs, comparative analysis).</li></ul>
        <h5>Hybrid (Watch + Ask)</h5><ul><li>User testing + interviews, contextual interviews, artifact-based methods.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"r-framework", title:"Research Framework Across the Lifecycle", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Assess</b> - interviews, surveys, comparative analysis.</li><li><b>Design</b> - lo-fi testing, hi-fi testing.</li><li><b>Build</b> - heuristic evaluation, analytics, observations.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"r-when", title:"When to Ask vs Observe vs Inspect", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Ask when</h5><ul><li>Observation is hard (rare, long, or private activities).</li><li>You need values, motivations and feelings.</li><li>You need large numbers and high certainty (surveys).</li></ul>
        <h5>Observe when</h5><ul><li>You need actual behaviour, not reported behaviour.</li><li>You want usability issues in real context.</li><li>You want to discover unexpected usage.</li></ul>
        <h5>Inspect when</h5><ul><li>You need a quick, cheap expert check.</li><li>You are benchmarking against standards or competitors.</li><li>You want likely flaws found before full user testing.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Quantitative research tells you the <b>what</b> and the proportion; qualitative tells you the <b>why</b> and the depth. Use surveys/analytics for "how many", interviews/observation for "why".</div>` },
      { id:"r-components", title:"The Four Components of UX", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Value</b> - Is it useful? Is it better than alternatives? (Does the design fulfil the need?)</li>
        <li><b>Usability</b> - Can users do what they need to do?</li>
        <li><b>Adoptability</b> - Is it easy to find and start using?</li>
        <li><b>Desirability</b> - Is it fun, attractive and pleasant?</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">VisiCalc (1979) had value despite a plain look. Motorola ROKR had value (music on a phone) but failed on usability and desirability. iPod (2001) won on all four. Duolingo onboarding is a strong adoptability example.</div>` },
      { id:"r-honeycomb", title:"Peter Morville's UX Honeycomb", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p>Seven factors that influence UX, with <b>Valuable</b> at the centre:</p>
        <ul><li><b>Useful</b> - serves a purpose.</li><li><b>Usable</b> - easy to use.</li><li><b>Desirable</b> - visually and emotionally appealing.</li><li><b>Findable</b> - can be located and navigated.</li><li><b>Accessible</b> - usable by people with disabilities.</li><li><b>Credible</b> - users trust it.</li><li><b>Valuable</b> - delivers value to user and business (centre).</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Map complaints to facets: "I didn't know it had an app" = Findable; "jobs are never in my field" = Useful; "it looks sketchy" = Credible; "no alt text" = Accessible.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 5. USER NEEDS & QUALITATIVE RESEARCH ---------------- */
  {
    section: "User Needs & Qualitative Research", id: "sec-needs", icon: "list", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Needs assessment, qualitative vs quantitative research, interview protocols, and affinity walls.",
    items: [
      { id:"n-needs", title:"User Needs Assessment", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A needs assessment finds out <b>why</b> people want a product, <b>how</b> they actually use it, what they like and dislike, and what else they want.</p>
        <h5>When</h5><ul><li><b>Before</b> any design or redesign.</li><li>Whenever questions or problems arise.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">In UX, "needs" means needs, wants, preferences and quirks. Usage data shows <b>where</b> users drop off; a needs assessment reveals <b>why</b> - which usage data alone cannot.</div>` },
      { id:"n-qual-quant", title:"Qualitative vs Quantitative Research", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Qualitative (interviews, observation, affinity walls)</h5><ul><li>Strengths: depth, broad use cases, hypotheses about what users want, causal explanations.</li><li>Non-numerical, direct contact, rich detail.</li><li>Weak for: proportions, confirming hunches, close comparisons.</li></ul>
        <h5>Quantitative (surveys, usage stats)</h5><ul><li>Good for overall trends and anticipated issues.</li><li>Weak for detailed understanding, new possibilities, unanticipated issues.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"n-interviews", title:"Semi-Structured Interviews", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A balance between the researcher's focus and free discussion. You use a <b>protocol</b> (list of questions) as a guide but expect to go off-script in a conversational style.</p>
        <h5>Who, where, when</h5><ul><li><b>Who</b> - a typical user or potential user.</li><li><b>Where</b> - where they normally use it and feel comfortable.</li><li><b>When</b> - scheduled, convenient, ideally when they normally use the product.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"n-protocol", title:"Interview Protocol Components", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Overarching question</b> - the big question you are trying to answer; kept in mind throughout but <b>not asked directly</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Introduction</b> - explain goals, duration, confidentiality, voluntary nature, permissions.</li>
        <li><b>Main interview</b> - short closed warm-up questions, then open-ended core questions.</li>
        <li><b>Observation</b> - think-aloud protocol and task prompts.</li>
        <li><b>Conclusion</b> - "anything to add?", thanks, follow-up plans, contact info.</li></ul>
        <p>Most important: interview <b>ethics</b>. Second: answering your overarching question.</p>` },
      { id:"n-questions", title:"Good vs Problematic Interview Questions", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Good</h5><ul><li><b>Open-ended:</b> "Why did you...?", "How did you go about...?", "Can you tell me about...?"</li><li><b>Specific/concrete:</b> "Tell me about the last time you did..."</li><li><b>Non-leading:</b> "What option do you prefer?"</li></ul>
        <h5>Problematic</h5><ul><li><b>Closed-ended:</b> "Do you like X?" (yes/no, limits the answer).</li><li><b>Abstract:</b> "What do you normally do when...?"</li><li><b>Leading:</b> "Why don't students use the library?" assumes the answer and adds bias.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Strongest needs-assessment question form: "Can you tell me about the last time you struggled to..., what happened?"</div>` },
      { id:"n-observe", title:"Observing Users & Think-Aloud", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Ask the user to do relevant tasks and to <b>think aloud</b> - say what they are doing and thinking. It is awkward at first; prompt gently with "What are you thinking now?". Watch for pauses, missteps and detours. The physical context tells you a lot even before the interview starts.</p>` },
      { id:"n-affinity", title:"Affinity Notes & Affinity Walls", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p>Soon after an interview, write <b>affinity notes</b>: a sticky note with a participant code (C01, G04...) plus a statement, quote, observation or question relevant to the assessment. Aim for about one note per minute of interview, each understandable on its own.</p>
        <h5>Building the wall</h5><ul><li>Cluster notes quickly; move them as patterns appear.</li><li>Aim for clusters of 3 to 7 notes.</li><li>Write higher-level summary notes that capture each cluster as a full sentence.</li><li>Stop adding levels when summaries become uninformative.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Condensing is understanding. Affinity walls are also called affinity diagrams or the KJ Method - a way to do qualitative analysis. The work is also known as ethnographic research or contextual inquiry.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 6. DESIGN THINKING ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Design Thinking & Human-Centered Design", id: "sec-dt", icon: "brain", scope: "in",
    blurb: "The five-stage design thinking process and its six guiding principles.",
    items: [
      { id:"dt-stages", title:"The 5 Stages of Design Thinking", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<ul><li><b>1. Empathize</b> - know your users and care about their lives.</li><li><b>2. Define</b> - frame the right problem (the only way to a right solution).</li><li><b>3. Ideate</b> - generate the broadest range of possibilities, not just the "right" idea.</li><li><b>4. Prototype</b> - build to think, test to learn.</li><li><b>5. Test</b> - a chance to learn about your solution and your user.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Skipping Empathize/Define (jumping straight to wireframes) risks solving the wrong problem.</div>` },
      { id:"dt-principles", title:"Six Design Thinking Principles", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Bias toward action</b> - favour doing and making.</li><li><b>Collaborate across boundaries</b> - mix disciplines and perspectives.</li><li><b>Focus on human values</b> - keep people at the centre.</li><li><b>Be mindful of process</b> - know which stage you are in.</li><li><b>Prototype toward a solution</b> - use prototypes to think and learn.</li><li><b>Show don't tell</b> - communicate visually and tangibly.</li></ul>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 7. DESIGN, PROTOTYPING & SKETCHING ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Design, Prototyping & Sketching", id: "sec-proto", icon: "layout", scope: "in",
    blurb: "The design process, prototype fidelity levels, premature commitment, sketching, and the sketch-prototype continuum.",
    items: [
      { id:"p-process", title:"The Design Process", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A cyclic, iterative sequence: <b>Understand the problem - Generate solutions - Analyze and select - Embody solutions - Assess (find new problems) - repeat.</b> It maps to <b>Assess - Design - Build</b>. Design is never truly finished.</p>` },
      { id:"p-special", title:"What's Special About UX Design", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Interactive systems are <b>time-based</b>: experiences unfold over time on <b>action-response rules</b>. Designing actions = the commands users have; designing responses = the information shown back.</p>
        <h5>Context shapes everything</h5><ul><li>Who, how, when, why (a student at a deadline vs an admin in office hours).</li><li>Other systems and devices in play.</li><li>Other activities at the same time (divided attention).</li><li>Other people present.</li><li>The assumed user vs the real user.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"p-why", title:"Why Prototype?", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A prototype is a representation of a design made <b>before</b> the final solution exists.</p>
        <ul><li>Turn abstract ideas into something testable.</li><li>Check whether the design meets goals or is going off track.</li><li>Give stakeholders something tangible to react to.</li><li>Maximise the number of times you revise before committing to expensive code.</li></ul>
        <p>Each prototype should answer one or more specific questions.</p>` },
      { id:"p-fidelity", title:"Fidelity Levels: Lo-Fi, Mid-Fi, Hi-Fi", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Fidelity, cost and time all rise together: Lo-Fi ($), Mid-Fi ($$), Hi-Fi ($$$).</p>
        <ul><li><b>Lo-Fi</b> - addresses functionality, basic organisation, task flow. Ignores graphics, fonts, colours, programming, real data.</li>
        <li><b>Mid-Fi</b> - addresses <b>layout, interactivity, navigation</b>. Still ignores graphics, fonts, colours, programming, real data.</li>
        <li><b>Hi-Fi</b> - addresses graphic design, interaction details, realistic data. Ignores backend programming and complete functional coverage (focuses on the specific task).</li></ul>` },
      { id:"p-premature", title:"Avoid Premature Commitment", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Premature commitment</b> means investing time and money into a design idea that turns out to be a dead end. It wastes resources and can leave you stuck with a bad design, because people find it hard to let go after investing a lot.</p>
        <div class="ex">Bill Moggridge: "Low fidelity prototypes are not precious and therefore invite more honest critique." Starting Lo-Fi means low investment and low emotional attachment, so feedback is more open and iteration is easier.</div>` },
      { id:"p-sketching", title:"Sketching", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A sketch is a cheap, fast representation of an idea: quick, timely, inexpensive, disposable, plentiful, minimal detail, allows ambiguity.</p>
        <h5>Why sketch</h5><ul><li>Create (early ideation, brainstorm lots of ideas), Record, Reflect, Share, Critique, Decide.</li></ul>
        <p>How: pencil/paper/whiteboard, go fast, make many, do not aim for perfect. You do <b>not</b> need to be good at drawing - sketching is about <b>design</b>, not art.</p>` },
      { id:"p-generation", title:"Generation vs Convergence (avoid fixation)", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Design swings between two forces: <b>Generation</b> (create and elaborate alternatives) and <b>Convergence</b> (reduce and select). These alternate repeatedly.</p>
        <div class="ex"><b>Local hill climbing:</b> improving one early idea without checking if a fundamentally better idea exists elsewhere. You reach a <b>local maximum</b> but miss the <b>global maximum</b>. Fix: generate many ideas and develop the best in parallel before converging.</div>` },
      { id:"p-sketch-vs-proto", title:"Sketch vs Prototype (a continuum)", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>The difference is mainly a <b>contrast of purpose</b> (and usually form). It is a continuum, not a switch.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Sketch:</b> evocative, suggest, explore, question, propose, tentative.</li><li><b>Prototype:</b> didactic, describe, refine, answer, test, specific.</li></ul>
        <p>Early on the criteria weight is on <b>design</b> (right ideas?); later it shifts to <b>usability</b> (does it work for users?).</p>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 8. CONCEPT TO WIREFRAME ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Concept to Wireframe (Personas, Scenarios, Rationale)", id: "sec-concept", icon: "scenario", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Framing wicked problems, personas and scenarios, storyboards, and the QOC design rationale framework.",
    items: [
      { id:"cw-wicked", title:"Wicked Problems & What Design Is", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Wicked problems</b> are ill-defined, have no right or wrong solution, are context-dependent, and have no clear test for solutions.</p>
        <p><b>Design</b> creates things that fulfil their <b>purpose</b> well, given <b>constraints</b>. Steve Jobs: design is "how it works", not just how it looks. Buxton: "Design is compromise."</p>
        <h5>Core design skills</h5><ul><li>Frame/reframe the problem; create alternatives; select among them; visualise and prototype; synthesise a solution that meets the important constraints.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"cw-framing", title:"Framing the Design Problem", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A <b>frame</b> is an active perspective that describes and changes a situation - what is in and what is out, the <b>scope</b> ("how big is the problem?") and the <b>design space</b> (what can vary). Framing is iterative: it shifts as timelines, budgets and understanding change.</p>` },
      { id:"cw-personas", title:"Personas", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Personas</b> are fictional characters representing key user types found through research. They are <b>based in data</b> and embody goals, traits, behaviours and contexts.</p>
        <h5>Elements</h5><ul><li>Demographics, goals and motivators, behaviours, context.</li></ul>
        <h5>Benefits</h5><ul><li>Summarise research, give a shortcut ("Would Mary do this?"), align the team on who users are, easy for non-designers, and fight the urge to design for yourself or an "elastic user".</li></ul>
        <div class="ex"><b>You are NOT your user.</b> Focus on common, not idiosyncratic, traits; keep content relevant.</div>` },
      { id:"cw-scenarios", title:"Design Scenarios", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Scenarios</b> are short stories about specific users using the technology to reach specific goals in a specific context. They bridge user needs and design ideas.</p>
        <h5>Elements</h5><ul><li>Setting, agents, goals, actions, events.</li></ul>
        <h5>Types</h5><ul><li>Problem, goal/task-based, activity, full-scale task, information, interaction scenarios.</li></ul>
        <p>You do not need to be a great writer; rough and quick beats few and polished.</p>` },
      { id:"cw-storyboards", title:"Storyboards (Big-Picture & Close-Up)", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p>A <b>storyboard</b> is a series of sketches showing how a user engages in an activity with the technology.</p>
        <h5>Two types</h5><ul><li><b>Big-picture storyboard</b> - focuses on user emotions, environment and motivation. Shows <b>WHY</b> users need the product.</li><li><b>Close-up storyboard</b> - focuses on screens, interactions and product use. Shows <b>WHAT</b> users do, screen by screen.</li></ul>
        <h5>Elements</h5><ul><li>Zoom, angle, detail, emotion, setting (and character, scene, plot, narrative).</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">A close-up storyboard would have caught a form asking for a "faculty staff ID" the student does not know; a big-picture storyboard captures emotion and context.</div>` },
      { id:"cw-qoc", title:"Design Rationale: Questions, Options, Criteria (QOC)", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Hard", scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>Design rationale</b> articulates and analyses trade-offs between alternatives to make principled decisions.</p>
        <ul><li><b>Questions</b> structure the design space and uncover alternatives.</li><li><b>Options</b> are different answers to a question.</li><li><b>Criteria</b> are required and desirable properties used to compare options.</li></ul>
        <p>Criteria come from formative work, usability (routine integration, privacy, social acceptability), prior studies, theory, and other design requirements. Evaluate each option against the criteria; the best contenders move forward.</p>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 9. WIREFRAME TO PROTOTYPE ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Wireframe to Prototype", id: "sec-wireframe", icon: "layout", scope: "in",
    blurb: "What wireframes answer, lo-fi interactive prototypes, how to run a lo-fi test, and fidelity trade-offs.",
    items: [
      { id:"w-wireframes", title:"Wireframes", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<p>A <b>wireframe</b> is a visual layout of a screen showing structure, the types of information shown, and pointer-based navigation - <b>no colour, images or branding</b>.</p>
        <h5>Wireframes answer</h5><ul><li>Do screens capture the right chunks of functionality? Are these the right components for one screen? Does the layout make sense? Are the right navigation elements present?</li></ul>
        <h5>Wireframes do NOT answer</h5><ul><li>How content should be ordered, how the user moves between screens (navigation underspecified), the right visual design, or non-visual output.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">They intentionally omit colour so stakeholders focus on <b>structure and information architecture</b> instead of aesthetic taste. Tools: pen and paper, Figma, Balsamiq.</div>` },
      { id:"w-vs-lofi", title:"Wireframe vs Low-Fi Prototype", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Wireframe</b> - very low fidelity, no interactivity, for layout and structure, a static document.</li><li><b>Low-Fi prototype</b> - low fidelity, basic clickability, for flow and usability testing, a clickable model.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"w-lofi-build", title:"Building a Lo-Fi Interactive Prototype", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Start with wireframes for each screen, create system responses (pop-ups, progress bars) for each action, add dummy realistic content, and make sure each action transitions to a state the prototype supports. Goal: make it "runnable" so stakeholders experience how it would behave. Lo-fi prototypes are cheap, disposable, and keep focus on concepts over details.</p>` },
      { id:"w-test-lofi", title:"Testing a Lo-Fi Prototype (roles & steps)", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Five steps</h5><ul><li>1. Select users - target audience or honest proxies.</li><li>2. Identify testing tasks - the prototype must support them fully, with fake but realistic data.</li><li>3. Find staff: <b>Facilitator</b> (instructs), <b>Computer</b> (changes prototype state), <b>Observer</b> (takes notes).</li><li>4. Conduct the test - give only clarification, let users figure it out, note where they get stuck.</li><li>5. Evaluate - find common problems, useful suggestions, and revise.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Basic usability problems appear after just 1 to 2 testers - revise before enrolling more. Let users explore first, and in the post-test interview ask about both the concept and specific issues.</div>` },
      { id:"w-fidelity-tradeoff", title:"Lo-Fi vs Hi-Fi: Advantages & Limits", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Low-fidelity</b> - cheap, tests many concepts, good for requirements and proof-of-concept. But limited error checking, facilitator-driven, weaker for detailed usability tests.</li>
        <li><b>High-fidelity</b> - complete functionality, fully interactive, user-driven, a living specification and sales tool. But resource-heavy, slow, and poor for early proof-of-concept or requirements gathering.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"w-wizard", title:"Wizard of Oz Prototypes", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"out",
        content:`<p>A prototype where a hidden human operator simulates the system's responses, so it looks functional to the user without real code.</p>
        <div class="section-note">This topic is <b>out of syllabus</b> for the final per instructor instructions. Kept for reference only.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 10. INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE & INTERFACE DESIGN ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Information Architecture & Interface Design", id: "sec-ia", icon: "layout", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Organising and labelling content, task vs user flows, and designing good error states.",
    items: [
      { id:"ia-basics", title:"What is Information Architecture (IA)?", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p><b>IA</b> is organising, structuring and labelling content so users can navigate and find information - the "blueprint" of a product.</p>
        <h5>Good IA improves</h5><ul><li>Findability, navigation, understanding, efficiency, satisfaction.</li></ul>
        <h5>Bad IA causes</h5><ul><li>Confusion, frustration, getting lost, higher cognitive load.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"ia-models", title:"3 IA Models (NN/g)", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Navigation model</b> - the visible structure users interact with (menus, nav bars, tabs).</li><li><b>Taxonomy model</b> - how content is grouped and categorised (product categories, tags).</li><li><b>Full IA structure</b> - the complete map of all pages and their relationships.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"ia-principles", title:"Core IA Principles & Labelling", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Organisation</b> - content arranged logically (group settings into Privacy, Notifications, Account).</li><li><b>Labelling</b> - clear, familiar names. Good: "Contact Us". Bad: "Connect Experience Portal".</li><li><b>Navigation</b> - users always know where they are, where they can go, how to return.</li><li><b>Search</b> - fast, accurate, with filters, for content-heavy platforms.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Organising by <b>user task</b> ("Renew a Licence") beats organising by internal <b>department</b> ("Transport Authority") because it matches the user's goal. Search does not replace IA: users must already know the right term to search, while good IA supports browsing and discovery.</div>` },
      { id:"ia-patterns", title:"IA Patterns", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Hierarchical</b> - tree structure; most common (Home - Products - Electronics - Phones).</li><li><b>Sequential</b> - step-by-step (checkout).</li><li><b>Matrix</b> - multiple paths (Netflix by genre, trending, search, recommendations).</li><li><b>Organic</b> - free exploration, no fixed path (art portfolios).</li></ul>` },
      { id:"ia-flows", title:"Task Flow vs User Flow", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<ul><li><b>Task flow</b> - the exact steps to complete ONE task. Linear, simple, <b>no branching</b>, no personas. Example: open app - select restaurant - add items - checkout - payment.</li>
        <li><b>User flow</b> - how a specific user type moves through a product. Includes <b>decisions</b>, multiple paths, entry and exit points, user goals. Example: if payment fails - retry - change method - cancel.</li></ul>
        <p>Flows help spot friction and unnecessary steps before UI design.</p>` },
      { id:"ia-error-state", title:"Error State Design", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Principles of good error states</h5><ul><li><b>Be specific</b> - "Password must be at least 8 characters", not "Error occurred".</li><li><b>Human language</b> - "You don't have permission", not "HTTP 403".</li><li><b>Offer a solution</b> - tell users what to do next.</li><li><b>Timing</b> - inline validation after the user finishes typing.</li><li><b>Don't rely on colour alone</b> - add icons, text and labels.</li></ul>
        <h5>Types</h5><ul><li>Form validation, empty states ("No messages yet"), network errors, permission errors, system errors.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">No feedback on a failed upload makes users tap repeatedly, creating duplicate posts - a missing error state plus missing system-status feedback.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 11. VISUAL DESIGN PRINCIPLES ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Visual Design Principles", id: "sec-visual", icon: "diagram", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Emphasis, hierarchy, scale, unity and variety - plus a Gestalt recap for visual grouping.",
    items: [
      { id:"v-principles", title:"Emphasis, Hierarchy, Scale, Unity, Variety", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in", diagram:true,
        content:`<ul><li><b>Emphasis</b> - makes an important element stand out (size, colour, contrast, placement) - CTA buttons.</li>
        <li><b>Hierarchy</b> - shows importance order so users read in an intended sequence (font size, weight, position) - large headlines first.</li>
        <li><b>Scale & proportion</b> - relative sizing to balance layouts and signal importance.</li>
        <li><b>Unity</b> - everything feels connected (consistent colour, type, spacing).</li>
        <li><b>Variety</b> - adds interest, but variety without enough unity feels chaotic.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">A news homepage with 14 identical cards lacks <b>visual hierarchy</b>, so users do not know where to begin. Fix with size, weight and emphasis differences.</div>` },
      { id:"v-gestalt-recap", title:"Gestalt Principles (Visual Recap)", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Proximity</b> - close items seem related (group a name with its phone number).</li><li><b>Similarity</b> - similar items seem connected.</li><li><b>Continuity</b> - eyes follow lines and paths.</li><li><b>Closure</b> - we mentally complete shapes (loading spinner read as a full circle).</li><li><b>Figure/Ground</b> - separating foreground from background (a modal over a dimmed page).</li></ul>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 12. HEURISTICS & GOLDEN RULES ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Heuristics & Golden Rules", id: "sec-heuristics", icon: "check", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Nielsen's 10 heuristics, Shneiderman's 8 golden rules, error prevention strategies and feedback mechanisms.",
    items: [
      { id:"h-nielsen", title:"Nielsen's 10 Usability Heuristics", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Broad rules of thumb for evaluating UI - the most-cited usability framework.</p>
        <ul><li><b>H1 Visibility of system status</b> - keep users informed (progress bar; "Step 3 of 5").</li>
        <li><b>H2 Match system and real world</b> - familiar words and conventions (trash-can icon, shopping cart).</li>
        <li><b>H3 User control and freedom</b> - clear exits, undo/redo (Gmail undo send).</li>
        <li><b>H4 Consistency and standards</b> - same words and actions mean the same thing (blue underline = link).</li>
        <li><b>H5 Error prevention</b> - design out problems (grey out invalid dates; "Are you sure?").</li>
        <li><b>H6 Recognition rather than recall</b> - make options visible (dropdowns, recent files).</li>
        <li><b>H7 Flexibility and efficiency</b> - accelerators for experts (keyboard shortcuts, autocomplete).</li>
        <li><b>H8 Aesthetic and minimalist design</b> - no irrelevant info (Google homepage).</li>
        <li><b>H9 Help users recognise, diagnose, recover from errors</b> - plain language, suggest a fix ("Incorrect password. Try again or reset it.").</li>
        <li><b>H10 Help and documentation</b> - easy to search, task-focused, concrete steps.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"h-shneiderman", title:"Shneiderman's 8 Golden Rules", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Actionable design rules (1986), best for designing interactive systems.</p>
        <ul><li><b>R1 Strive for consistency.</b></li><li><b>R2 Seek universal usability</b> - serve novices to experts, all ages and abilities (font controls, alt text).</li><li><b>R3 Offer informative feedback</b> - feedback for every action ("Saved!" toast).</li><li><b>R4 Design dialogs to yield closure</b> - beginning, middle, end ("Order confirmed!").</li><li><b>R5 Prevent errors</b> - and help recovery (disable Submit until fields are filled).</li><li><b>R6 Permit easy reversal of actions</b> - undo to relieve anxiety and encourage exploration.</li><li><b>R7 Keep users in control.</b></li><li><b>R8 Reduce short-term memory load</b> - keep displays simple; carry data forward across pages.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"h-compare", title:"Nielsen vs Shneiderman", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Nielsen:</b> 10 principles, broader/abstract, used for <b>evaluation after design</b> (heuristic evaluation), published 1990/1994.</li>
        <li><b>Shneiderman:</b> 8 rules, more prescriptive/specific, used as <b>design guidelines during development</b>, published 1986.</li></ul>
        <p>They reinforce each other: R1-R4-consistency, R3-H1-feedback, R5-H5-prevention, R6-H3-reversal, R8-H6-recognition. Use them together.</p>` },
      { id:"h-prevention", title:"Error Prevention Strategies", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>P1 Constrain inputs</b> - dropdowns, date pickers, masks instead of free text.</li><li><b>P2 Inline validation</b> - check as the user types; positive guidance (password strength meter).</li><li><b>P3 Confirmation for destructive actions</b> - friction proportional to consequence (modal for "delete all", not for "change font").</li><li><b>P4 Affordances and mapping</b> - controls look like their function; grouping matches behaviour.</li><li><b>P5 Make modes visible</b> - persistent indicators, not just tooltips (Caps Lock indicator).</li><li><b>P6 Undo and graceful recovery</b> - multi-level undo, clear messages, never silently lose data.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Confirming <b>every</b> action (even resizing an image) trains users to click "Yes" blindly - that breaks friction proportionality.</div>` },
      { id:"h-feedback", title:"Feedback Mechanisms", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Feedback closes the loop between action and response (Nielsen H1, Shneiderman R3). It should be timely, relevant and proportional.</p>
        <h5>By response time</h5><ul><li><b>Immediate</b> 0-100ms - button clicks (press animation).</li><li><b>Short-term</b> 100ms-1s - search, validation.</li><li><b>Long-term</b> 1s-10s - uploads, API calls (progress bar).</li><li><b>Background</b> >10s - exports (notification badge).</li></ul>
        <h5>Modalities</h5><ul><li>Visual (colour, animation, toasts) and non-visual (sound, haptic, screen-reader, push).</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Feedback should match the action's importance. Over-notifying trains people to ignore alerts - the <b>cry-wolf anti-pattern</b>.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 13. EVALUATION & TESTING ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Evaluation & Usability Testing", id: "sec-eval", icon: "target", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Heuristic evaluation, severity ratings, formative vs summative, and how to plan and run a usability test.",
    items: [
      { id:"e-heuristic-eval", title:"Heuristic Evaluation", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>A usability <b>inspection</b> method (1990): experts judge a UI against recognised principles. A "discount" method - cheap, fast, expert-driven, not user-driven.</p>
        <h5>Process</h5><ul><li>Brief evaluators (10-15 min); each inspects alone (1-2 hrs); walk the UI at least twice; document violations citing the heuristic; aggregate and rate severity; prioritise fixes and report.</li></ul>
        <p>Output: a <b>list of violations with severity ratings</b>. About <b>3 to 5 evaluators</b> is enough.</p>
        <div class="ex">Limits: expert bias (may miss real user issues), false positives (reported non-problems), does not reveal unknown unknowns, and quality depends on evaluator skill.</div>` },
      { id:"e-severity", title:"Nielsen's Severity Rating Scale", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>0</b> - not a usability problem; no action.</li><li><b>1</b> - cosmetic; fix if time permits.</li><li><b>2</b> - minor; low priority, next iteration.</li><li><b>3</b> - major; high priority fix.</li><li><b>4</b> - catastrophe; must fix before release.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">A logout buried three menus deep is roughly a 3 (major). A core task with 40% completion where most users give up is a 4 (catastrophe).</div>` },
      { id:"e-formative-summative", title:"Formative vs Summative Evaluation", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Scriven (1967): "When the cook tastes the soup, that's formative; when the guests taste it, that's summative." <b>Formative improves; summative proves.</b></p>
        <ul><li><b>Formative</b> - during design, to find and fix problems. Small (5-8), qualitative. Methods: think-aloud, heuristic eval, cognitive walkthrough, paper prototyping.</li>
        <li><b>Summative</b> - after design, to measure performance. Larger (30-100+), quantitative. Methods: usability benchmark, SUS survey, analytics.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">At wireframe stage you need <b>formative</b> evaluation now; waiting until after launch is like tasting the soup only when it is served - too late to change the recipe cheaply.</div>` },
      { id:"e-usability-basics", title:"Usability Testing Basics", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Testing a product with <b>representative users</b> doing <b>real tasks</b> while researchers observe.</p>
        <ul><li>85% of usability problems are found with just <b>5 users</b> (Nielsen). You need more test rounds, not more users.</li><li>$1 spent on UX returns about $100 in value.</li><li>70% of online businesses fail due to poor usability.</li></ul>
        <h5>Formative vs summative testing</h5><ul><li>Formative - during design, 3-5 users, qualitative, improve.</li><li>Summative - after design, 20+ users, quantitative, benchmark.</li></ul>
        <p>Also split as moderated vs unmoderated, in-person vs remote, explorative vs assessment.</p>` },
      { id:"e-moderated", title:"Moderated vs Unmoderated", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Moderated</b> - facilitator present, can probe with follow-ups, higher cost, small sample (5-10), rich qualitative. Best for complex tasks and early stages.</li>
        <li><b>Unmoderated</b> - no facilitator, user's own environment, no real-time clarification, lower cost, large sample (50-100+), behavioural data. Best for confirmatory testing.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"e-planning", title:"Planning a Usability Test", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li>1. Define goals (e.g. "can users check out in under 2 minutes?").</li><li>2. Recruit 5-8 representative users matching the persona.</li><li>3. Create realistic task scenarios.</li><li>4. Choose method and tools (Maze, UserTesting, paper).</li><li>5. Prepare the protocol - script, consent, equipment, pilot test with one person.</li><li>6. Run and observe - stay neutral, record time, errors, expressions.</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Testing a hospital-patient app with healthy university students is invalid: participants do not match the target users (elderly, post-surgery, limited mobility).</div>` },
      { id:"e-tasks", title:"Writing Good Task Scenarios", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>Rules</h5><ul><li>Use realistic scenarios, not instructions.</li><li>Don't give away the answer or use UI-specific terms ("shopping cart").</li><li>Avoid jargon; include a clear end condition; pilot test first.</li></ul>
        <h5>Bad vs good</h5><ul><li>Bad: "Click the Shop button." Good: "You want to buy a birthday gift for your sister - find something under Rs. 2000."</li></ul>
        <h5>Common task pitfalls</h5><ul><li>Leading the witness, ambiguity, no context/motivation, not telling users when to stop, not pilot testing.</li></ul>
        <p>Task types: <b>closed-ended</b> (clear endpoint, verifiable) vs <b>open-ended</b> (user judges completion, exploratory). Progress from easy to hard; avoid ordering effects.</p>` },
      { id:"e-think-aloud", title:"The Think-Aloud Protocol", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Users say their thoughts aloud as they work - the most widely used usability method.</p>
        <h5>How to run it</h5><ul><li>Ask them to "say whatever comes to mind".</li><li>If silent, prompt gently: "What are you thinking right now?".</li><li><b>Never</b> answer questions about the interface during the task; let them struggle and note where/how long.</li></ul>
        <p>The golden rule: <b>the user is never wrong - the design is.</b> Reveals confusion, mental models, the words users use, emotions, and <b>why</b> errors happen.</p>
        <div class="ex">Helping a struggling user invalidates the data. If a facilitator points to the back arrow, that finding is contaminated - they should reflect the question back instead.</div>` },
      { id:"e-metrics", title:"Usability Metrics (PULSE) & SUS", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<h5>PULSE framework</h5><ul><li><b>P</b>erformance - completion rate, time on task, error rate.</li><li><b>U</b>sage - how often, which features.</li><li><b>L</b>earnability - time to first success.</li><li><b>S</b>atisfaction - SUS, NPS, questionnaires.</li><li><b>E</b>rrors - frequency, severity, recovery time.</li></ul>
        <p><b>SUS (System Usability Scale):</b> 10 questions, scored 0-100. Above <b>68</b> = above average.</p>
        <div class="ex">A 70% completion rate plus double the benchmark time means users <b>can</b> finish but the flow is inefficient and full of friction - they are getting through, but struggling.</div>` },
      { id:"e-analyze", title:"Analyzing & Reporting Results", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Capture <b>critical incidents</b>: errors, expressed frustration, breakdowns, pleasant surprises. Success is a spectrum (did they finish? how much help? how long?). Steps: compile observations, find recurring issues, rate severity, prioritise fixes, write recommendations. Document immediately - you lose about half of insights from memory alone.</p>
        <h5>Common mistakes</h5><ul><li>Testing the user not the design, helping during tasks, testing with team members, too many tasks (keep to 60-90 min, 5-8 tasks), no clear goals, poor documentation.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"e-ethics", title:"Ethics in User Testing", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li>Participation is voluntary; no coercion.</li><li>Participants can stop at any time.</li><li>You are testing the <b>system</b>, not the participant - they should never feel judged.</li><li>Communicate all of this before starting.</li></ul>` },
      { id:"e-ab", title:"A/B Testing", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Medium", scope:"out",
        content:`<p>A quantitative method comparing version A (control) and B (variant) with real users to see which hits a target metric better.</p>
        <div class="section-note">This topic is <b>out of syllabus</b> for the final per instructor instructions. Kept for reference only.</div>` },
      { id:"e-micro", title:"Micro-Usability (\"Hallway\") Testing", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"out",
        content:`<p>A lightweight, informal usability test: relaxed recruiting, fewer tasks, under 30 minutes, little or no formal data collection.</p>
        <div class="section-note">The micro-usability testing video lectures are <b>out of syllabus</b> for the final per instructor instructions. Kept for reference only.</div>` }
    ]
  },

  /* ---------------- 14. ACCESSIBILITY & INCLUSIVE DESIGN ---------------- */
  {
    section: "Accessibility & Inclusive Design", id: "sec-access", icon: "eye", scope: "in",
    blurb: "Accessible vs inclusive design, the curb-cut effect, and an accessibility audit checklist.",
    items: [
      { id:"a-vs", title:"Accessible vs Inclusive Design", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Accessible design</b> - usable by people with disabilities; removes barriers; uses WCAG (screen-reader support, keyboard navigation, alt text). <b>Reactive, compliance-focused.</b></li>
        <li><b>Inclusive design</b> - designs for diverse human experiences from the start (age, language, culture, gender, economic status, ability). <b>Proactive, human-focused.</b></li></ul>
        <div class="ex">A design can be accessible but not inclusive - meeting compliance yet still ignoring, say, low-literacy or low-bandwidth users.</div>` },
      { id:"a-curbcut", title:"The Curb-Cut Effect", priority:"high", difficulty:"Easy", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Accessibility features often help <b>everyone</b>. A wheelchair ramp also helps parents with strollers, cyclists and delivery workers. Captions help people in noisy places. Designing for accessibility brings a larger audience, better usability, ethics and better business outcomes.</p>` },
      { id:"a-color", title:"Don't Rely on Colour Alone", priority:"high", difficulty:"Medium", examLikely:true, scope:"in",
        content:`<p>Using only red/green to signal status fails users with red-green colour blindness. A complete fix is <b>multi-layered</b>: add text labels ("Danger"/"Safe"), different icon shapes (triangle vs circle), and WCAG-compliant contrast - so colour is never the only signal.</p>` },
      { id:"a-audit", title:"Accessibility Audit Checklist", priority:"normal", difficulty:"Easy", scope:"in",
        content:`<ul><li><b>Visual</b> - is contrast OK? Is text scalable?</li><li><b>Keyboard</b> - fully navigable without a mouse?</li><li><b>Screen reader</b> - labels and alt text on all elements?</li><li><b>Forms</b> - errors clear and descriptive; labels stay visible while typing?</li><li><b>Colour use</b> - any colour-only signals?</li><li><b>Motion</b> - excessive animation?</li></ul>
        <div class="ex">Touch targets of 18x18 px and a 2.8:1 contrast ratio both fail accessibility - targets should be large (about 44px) and contrast should meet WCAG.</div>` }
    ]
  }
];

